I'm Kathryn manga award for reason TV  and i'm here with reason foundation  polling director Emily Ekins who's going  to break down our second reason roop  pull thanks for joining me Emily thanks  for having me so let's start with the  headline which is we're ten years after  911 and Americans have some opinions  about that well we found that eighty  percent of americans believe that the  security measures implemented after the  911 attacks have made us safer overall  but at the same time that has come with  some costs so for example fifty five  percent of americans say that we've  given up too much freedom and privacy in  the name of security so one of the big  questions post 9-11 is whether there'll  be another attack and the bureaucratic  entity tasked with preventing that is  the Department of the Homeland Security  do americans think that we are  well-positioned ten years out well  interestingly we only found that fifteen  percent of americans are very confident  that the department of homeland security  will prevent another terrorist attack  from occurring on US soil but then forty  percent said that they were somewhat  somewhat confident but still they are  definitely unclear as to whether the  department of homeland security will be  able to achieve its main goal the other  frontline player and security is the  Transportation Security Administration  which is an entity that everyone loves  to hate let's talk about what people  told you about their attitudes about the  TSA the public's evenly divided about  when it comes to whether the TSA will be  able to stop a terrorist trying to board  a US or a plane at a u.s. airport but  interestingly if you look at Democrats  versus Republicans a majority of  Democrats trust that the TSA would  prevent a terrorist from boarding a  plane but conversely a majority of  Republicans don't agree the policy  solution favored by many libertarians in  response to some outcry against  incursions on personal space and privacy  by the TSA has  to privatize it what did you find when  you ask people about that solution well  neither those who favored or oppose that  kind of reform reached in majority 47%  opposed forty-one percent favored but we  did find that a majority of Democrats  opposed privatizing the TSA whereas a  majority of Republicans favored such a  reform ten years out were also still at  war the u.s. is a major presence in  Afghanistan and Iraq and at least  initially the justification for going  into Afghanistan was that it would make  Americans safer what do Americans think  about that attitude now Americans do not  seem to be convinced that being in  Afghanistan and staying in Afghanistan  will make us safer only nineteen percent  said that they believed the u.s. staying  in afghanistan until 2014 at least would  make a safer but sixty-five percent  thought it would really have no impact  and what about in iraq are people  positive on that venture no Americans  are not happy about the war in Iraq was  sixty-two percent thing that it was not  worth it for the u.s. to go in what is  your final finding in this poll in which  you asked about whether the US  government should mind its own business  well essentially that is kind of the  general sentiment that we learn from  this poll is that Americans generally  are favoring a policy where the u.s.  mines its own business we asked when it  came to international military  interventions whether or not the US uses  it too much the right amount not enough  and overwhelmingly 61 percent said the  u.s. uses military force in foreign  conflicts too often thank you very much  Emily for reason TV I'm Catherine manga  award  you
 

